\paragraph{Code World Models and Execution Simulation.}
Code World Models~\cite{meta2025cwm} mid-train a 32B LLM on execution traces to predict output strings, establishing the generation-based paradigm for execution-free verification.
Self-Execution~\cite{maimon2026selfexecution} extends this with step-by-step trace prediction; CodeMind~\cite{liu2024codemind} evaluates execution prediction as one dimension of code reasoning; SolidCoder~\cite{lee2026solidcoder} bridges mental simulation and actual execution; Abdollahi et al.~\cite{abdollahi2025demystifying} identify systematic failure modes in LLM trace prediction. All share a generation formulation whose cost scales with output length. We provide the first controlled comparison of this formulation against classification and trajectory-level alternatives at matched capacity.

\paragraph{Execution-free Verification.}
SWE-RM~\cite{shum2025swerm} trains a trajectory-level reward model for patch ranking; Patch Reasoner~\cite{xu2025patchreasoner} uses chain-of-thought for patch verification; LEVER~\cite{ni2023lever} and CodeT~\cite{chen2023codet} train classifiers for execution correctness prediction. ICE-Score~\cite{zhuo2024icescore}, CodeScore~\cite{dong2024codescore}, and CodeBERTScore~\cite{zhou2023codebertscore} evaluate code quality via learned metrics. Snell et al.~\cite{snell2024scaling} show test-time scaling via verification outperforms parameter scaling; S*~\cite{li2025sstar} and VG-Search~\cite{chen2025vgsearch} find finer-grained verification improves selection. Self-consistency~\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} uses majority voting as an implicit verifier. These works treat verification granularity as a design choice but do not compare formulations at controlled capacity.

\paragraph{Process vs.\ Outcome Supervision.}
Lightman et al.~\cite{lightman2023letsverify} and Uesato et al.~\cite{uesato2022solving} show process-level verification outperforms outcome-level for mathematical reasoning. In code, DreamPRM-Code~\cite{dreamprm2025code} and PRL-Coder~\cite{ye2025prlcoder} apply process supervision to generation. Our per-test classification is structurally analogous: each test verdict is an intermediate verification step. Our TOST equivalence result ($p = 0.007$) confirms per-test achieves equivalent accuracy to trajectory-level with additional diagnostic value.

\paragraph{Verification in Repair and Mutation Testing.}
CodeRL~\cite{le2022coderl}, AlphaCode~\cite{li2022alphacode,alphacode2023team}, and Reflexion~\cite{shinn2023reflexion} use execution feedback for iterative code improvement. SWE-bench~\cite{jimenez2024swebench} and SWE-bench Verified~\cite{chowdhury2024swebenchverified} provide standard benchmarks; SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent} and Agentless~\cite{xia2024agentless} demonstrate repair pipelines where Yang et al.~\cite{yang2025aprsurvey} identify verification as a persistent cost bottleneck. Predictive mutation testing~\cite{tufano2019predictive,kim2022predictive,wang2024llmmutation,jain2023contextual} shares our per-test problem structure. Our formulation comparison is complementary to these systems: verifier efficiency and granularity directly affect viability at scale.

\paragraph{PEFT and Task Framing.}
LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} and QLoRA~\cite{dettmers2024qlora} enable parameter-efficient fine-tuning; DeepSeek-Coder~\cite{guo2024deepseekcoder} shows adapter-based training preserves pretrained code understanding. We use LoRA (rank 16) for all experiments so capacity differences reflect task structure; our rank ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}) confirms doubling capacity does not resolve the generation bottleneck. More broadly, task framing affects model performance~\cite{raffel2020t5,lewis2020bart,rajpurkar2016squad}; we extend this lens to code verification, finding complexity moderates the tradeoff in a model-contingent manner.

